'''''Spiraea × vanhouttei''''', called '''Van Houtte's spiraea''', '''Vanhoutte spirea''', or '''bridal wreath''', is an artificial [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] species of flowering plant in the family [[Rosaceae]].<ref name="RHS_97141" >{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/97141/spiraea-x-vanhouttei/details |title=RHS Plant Profile ''Spiraea'' × ''vanhouttei'' Van Houtte's spiraea |author=<!--Not stated-->|date=2026 |website=Find a plant |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=30 June 2026 |quote=Other common names; bridal wreath ... 7 suppliers }}</ref> Its parents are ''[[Spiraea cantoniensis]]'', Reeves' spirea, (from southeastern China) and ''[[Spiraea trilobata]]'', the Asian meadowsweet, (from Central Asia, southern Siberia, and northern China).<ref name="POWO_1011556-1" >{{cite POWO |id=1011556-1 |title=''Spiraea × vanhouttei'' (Briot) Carrière |access-date=30 June 2026 }}</ref> A vigorous deciduous shrub reaching {{cvt|2|m|ft|0}}, it is typically found in human-influenced situations such as old hedges, homesteads, and alongside roads at elevations from sea level to {{cvt|2000|m}}.<ref name="FNA_242322736" >{{cite web |url=http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=242417313 |title=17. ''Spiraea'' ×''vanhouttei'' (Briot) Carrière, Rev. Hort. 48: 260. 1876. (as species) Bridal-wreath |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2026 |website=Flora of North America |publisher=Missouri Botanical Garden & Harvard University Herbaria |access-date=30 June 2026 }}</ref> Although considered [[Introduced species|introduced]] in Europe and the eastern United States, it appears to be sterile.<ref name="FNA_242322736" />

==In cultivation==
The original cross was made by [[Charles Billiard]] at [[Fontenay-aux-Roses]] and released to the public in 1862, and was popularized by [[Louis van Houtte]].<ref name="Origin" >{{cite web |last1=Klingaman |first1=Gerald |date=7 March 2003 |title=Plant of the Week Vanhoutte Spirea |url=https://www.uaex.uada.edu/yard-garden/resource-library/plant-week/vanhoutte-spiraea.aspx |website=Plant of the Week |location=Little Rock, AR |publisher=University of Arkansas Division of Agriculture |access-date=2 July 2026 }}</ref> Within a century it was thought to be the most widely planted deciduous shrub in the US.<ref name="Widely" >{{cite journal |last1=Hildreth |first1=A. C. |date=February 1951 |title=Ornamental Shrubs for the Southern Great Plains |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=PfgvIojnVN4C |journal=Farmers' Bulletin |volume=2025 |issue= |publisher=United States Department of Agriculture |pages=62 |access-date=2 July 2026 }}</ref> Hardy to [[Hardiness zone|USDA zone]] 3a, best results are had by pruning any stems that have turned woody back to ground level after the April to May flowering period.<ref name="Widely" /><ref name="MBG_253831" >{{cite web |url=https://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?taxonid=253831 |title=''Spiraea'' × ''vanhouttei'' |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=2026 |website=Plant Finder |publisher=Missouri Botanical Garden |access-date=2 July 2026 }}</ref> There are a number of [[cultivar]]s, with 'Gold Fountain' and 'Pink Ice' being readily available from commercial nurseries.<ref name="RHS_151711" >{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/151711/spiraea-x-vanhouttei-gold-fountain/details |title=RHS Plant Profile ''Spiraea'' × ''vanhouttei'' 'Gold Fountain' Van Houtte's spiraea 'Gold Fountain' |author=<!--Not stated-->|date=2026 |website=Find a plant |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=2 July 2026 |quote=semi-evergreen ... 8 suppliers }}</ref><ref name="RHS_202813" >{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/202813/spiraea-x-vanhouttei-pink-ice-v/details |title=RHS Plant Profile ''Spiraea'' × ''vanhouttei'' 'Pink Ice' (v) |author=<!--Not stated-->|date=2026 |website=Find a plant |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=2 July 2026 |quote=Synonyms; ''Spiraea bullata'' 'Pink Ice' ''Spiraea japonica'' 'Pink Ice' ''Spiraea'' × ''vanhouttei'' 'Catpan' ... 8 suppliers }}</ref>

